1
00:00:31,434 --> 00:00:32,796
Good evening, everyone.

2
00:00:32,896 --> 00:00:34,598
I trust you had a fruitful day.

3
00:00:34,719 --> 00:00:35,701
I do not say happy.

4
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:37,283
I hope it has been happy.

5
00:00:37,323 --> 00:00:54,826
Namo Tassa Bhagavato Harahato Sambha Sambho Tassa Namo Tassa Bhagavato Harahato Sambha Sambho Tassa

6
00:00:55,616 --> 00:01:04,585
Namo dasa bhagavato arhato sambha sambuddhasa.

7
00:01:04,605 --> 00:01:17,018
Homage to the Buddha, the blessed, noble and fully self-awakened one.

8
00:01:17,058 --> 00:01:25,506
So I thought we'd move on to a different discourse, but touching upon a similar theme,

9
00:01:25,790 --> 00:01:30,495
with one or two things to be learnt, and then it probably takes two evenings.

10
00:01:31,477 --> 00:01:36,763
So the Buddha is now in the Kasi country.

11
00:01:37,706 --> 00:01:42,090
He's got a large sangha of bhikkhus, and he's talking to them.

12
00:01:42,191 --> 00:01:45,316
So this is what he said in the earlier discourse.

13
00:01:45,756 --> 00:01:54,867
So remember the Buddha, he has to keep repeating things, mainly either because he couldn't get one or he didn't have the money.

14
00:01:55,152 --> 00:01:57,995
to buy a digital recorder.

15
00:01:58,334 --> 00:02:02,539
So everywhere he goes, he has to repeat everything.

16
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:07,043
So he says, I abstain from eating at night.

17
00:02:07,983 --> 00:02:14,691
By doing so, I'm free from illness and affliction and I enjoy health, strength and a comfortable abiding.

18
00:02:15,872 --> 00:02:18,413
Come bhikkhus, abstain from eating at night.

19
00:02:18,593 --> 00:02:22,358
By doing so, you too will be free from illness and affliction

20
00:02:22,522 --> 00:02:25,447
and will enjoy health, strength and comfortable abiding.

21
00:02:25,926 --> 00:02:36,319
Now, then he went wandering in stages until he came to the city of Kitagiri, which is still in the Kazi country.

22
00:02:37,361 --> 00:02:43,889
So now on that occasion, there were two monks called Asaji and Punabasuko who were residing there.

23
00:02:45,219 --> 00:03:04,743
And some of the Bhikkhus who were there went to see them and, you know, the usual thing of exchanging courteous greetings, said to them, you know, from now on, the Master, the Buddha, the Blessed One does not want us to eat in the evening, you see.

24
00:03:05,966 --> 00:03:07,987
So now the two Bhikkhus said, friends.

25
00:03:09,349 --> 00:03:15,176
Now, remember that the rule about not eating after midday was also

26
00:03:15,324 --> 00:03:43,358
established you see friends they said we eat in the evening in the morning and in the day outside the proper time in other words we're not obeying this rule by doing so we are free from illness and affliction and we enjoy health strength and comfortable abiding why should we abandon a benefit visible here and now to pursue a benefit to be achieved at a future times so

27
00:03:43,742 --> 00:03:48,268
We shall eat in the evening, in the morning, and in the day outside the proper time.

28
00:03:49,128 --> 00:03:50,550
So this, of course, was mutiny.

29
00:03:52,992 --> 00:03:59,460
Now, since the bhikkhus were unable to convince those bhikkhus, Asaji and Punabhasuka, they went to the Blessed One.

30
00:03:59,659 --> 00:04:00,461
They snitched on them.

31
00:04:03,844 --> 00:04:06,828
And, of course, they told the Buddha that these two monks were not behaving.

32
00:04:06,867 --> 00:04:08,289
Now, they were naughty.

33
00:04:08,330 --> 00:04:09,531
They were very naughty monks.

34
00:04:10,270 --> 00:04:12,711
Let me see if I can get the note on this.

35
00:04:17,997 --> 00:04:18,757
It's 697.

36
00:04:20,038 --> 00:04:24,903
And I'll tell you what it says about them.

37
00:04:24,923 --> 00:04:26,223
This is the commentary, of course.

38
00:04:28,607 --> 00:04:39,096
So they were unscrupulous and depraved and showed indulging in various kinds of bad conduct that bring about the corruption of the laity.

39
00:04:39,952 --> 00:04:43,677
In other words, they had a bad example and the lay people lose their faith.

40
00:04:45,139 --> 00:04:57,456
And actually later on at Kitagari, an act of banishment was pronounced against them and their refusal to obey led to the promulgation of the Sangha Disesa 13, which I will read in a bit.

41
00:05:01,201 --> 00:05:06,788
So anyway, the Buddha does call for them to see him.

42
00:05:07,324 --> 00:05:19,276
And he asks them, is it true that when a number of bhikkhus went and told you about this new rule, you said we shall eat in the evening, in the morning and in the daytime at the proper time?

43
00:05:19,716 --> 00:05:22,439
Yes, venerable sir.

44
00:05:22,459 --> 00:05:29,365
Now, he says, have you ever known me to teach dharma in this way?

45
00:05:29,865 --> 00:05:36,892
Whatever a person experiences, whether pleasant or painful or neutral, wholesome,

46
00:05:37,278 --> 00:05:41,264
Unwholesome states diminish in him and wholesome states increase.

47
00:05:41,904 --> 00:05:53,180
So the operative word is whatever this person experiences, whatever this person experiences, they say no, they understand the subtlety here.

48
00:05:53,201 --> 00:06:04,838
So here the Buddha says, when someone feels a certain kind of pleasant feeling, unwholesome states increase in him and wholesome states decrease.

49
00:06:05,627 --> 00:06:14,536
And when someone feels another kind of pleasant feeling, unwholesome states diminish in him and wholesome states increase.

50
00:06:15,298 --> 00:06:28,572
So in other words, there's always this distinction between the worldly, the sensual, the indulgence, and the world of the spiritual life, which includes renunciation, but also the benefits of renunciation.

51
00:06:30,553 --> 00:06:34,538
We'll come to that again, I think, towards the end of this evening.

52
00:06:35,345 --> 00:06:37,588
So much the same way as painful feelings.

53
00:06:37,608 --> 00:06:43,216
So some painful feelings, unwholesome states increase and wholesome states diminish.

54
00:06:44,458 --> 00:06:51,026
And when someone has another kind of painful feeling, unwholesome states diminish and wholesome states increase.

55
00:06:51,908 --> 00:07:02,583
So sometimes when we have painful feelings, these are based on a lack of something that we want and it increases our desire for something.

56
00:07:02,723 --> 00:07:03,764
So that's the attachment.

57
00:07:04,353 --> 00:07:18,252
But on the other hand, those unpleasant feelings we have when we have to relinquish something, abandon something, that toast in the evening, then that, of course, is something which makes us grow.

58
00:07:18,271 --> 00:07:22,218
And the same can be said of neutral feelings.

59
00:07:25,541 --> 00:07:33,533
So if it were known to me, unseen, unfound, unrealized, uncontactable by wisdom,

60
00:07:34,711 --> 00:07:42,644
When someone feels a certain kind of pleasant feeling, unwholesome states increase in him and wholesome states diminish, would that be fitting?

61
00:07:42,704 --> 00:07:57,971
So now he goes through this whole thing again, asking, you know, if I hadn't known this to be true for myself, if it wasn't something that I had realized, that I had found, that I had contacted, would I be teaching this?

62
00:07:58,312 --> 00:08:00,194
And of course they say no.

63
00:08:02,673 --> 00:08:17,494
So then he says to go through those different things, the pleasant and unpleasant and neutral feelings, which are associated both with the sensual life, the indulgent life and with nunciate life.

64
00:08:18,134 --> 00:08:24,863
And one of them will bring increase in our spiritual benefit and the other one will be decrease.

65
00:08:25,384 --> 00:08:27,447
So he says all this is known by me.

66
00:08:28,795 --> 00:08:37,206
When it comes to neutral feeling, of course, it's a more subtle thing, it's the distinction between being indifferent and being equanimous.

67
00:08:38,368 --> 00:08:53,587
So sometimes we don't have much of a feeling for something, but when we look into it, it's basically an indifference and not pure equanimity.

68
00:08:55,475 --> 00:09:02,066
Now, just before we go on to the next bit, let's read this Sangha Disesa.

69
00:09:02,166 --> 00:09:08,135
So the Buddha is very concerned with the way lay people react to the order.

70
00:09:08,336 --> 00:09:13,284
I mean, obviously, the order has now become highly dependent on lay people.

71
00:09:13,384 --> 00:09:16,169
That's the closeness of it.

72
00:09:17,631 --> 00:09:21,177
Monks and nuns in the Theravada tradition

73
00:09:21,495 --> 00:09:49,274
are dependent on the support of lay people so they can't work they can't make money etc etc and this creates a sort of dependency which allows also for a spiritual intimacy so the lay people will come to the monasteries uh for blessings and for teachings and for meditation and in return uh will and out of gratitude as it were will offer the monks their needs

74
00:09:50,369 --> 00:10:16,782
can also be done out of compassion because in a sense you could see monasteries and meditation centers as a spiritual hospitals right this is where you go when you feel spiritually sick and you want to get better so this is the uh sangha de sesa now there are many there are different types of rules but the the the most serious one is called a paragika which means a defeat

75
00:10:16,879 --> 00:10:22,687
And this is when a monk or nun loses their robe because of a serious offense.

76
00:10:23,649 --> 00:10:31,200
They tend to be anything to do with murder or inciting somebody to suicide, anything like that.

77
00:10:31,980 --> 00:10:37,428
Stealing, of course, anything that a local authority or authority would put you in jail for.

78
00:10:38,289 --> 00:10:43,157
Any sexual misconduct, any sexual intercourse at all.

79
00:10:43,475 --> 00:10:55,649
And finally, anything that breaks up the Sangha, causing a schism in the Sangha, in the actual monks, in the community of monks and nuns.

80
00:10:56,029 --> 00:11:00,575
But then there's another sect called Sanghadisesa, and there are 13 of these.

81
00:11:00,635 --> 00:11:07,744
And for this, if a monk breaks one of these rules or a nun, they have to confess to five

82
00:11:07,909 --> 00:11:16,462
of the five monastics, the monks to the monks and the women to the women.

83
00:11:16,482 --> 00:11:22,552
And then they have to sit, they have to stay apart from the order for six nights.

84
00:11:23,052 --> 00:11:30,803
And when I say apart, it really means that the order is not supposed to communicate to them and they cannot communicate with the order.

85
00:11:30,823 --> 00:11:35,971
So in a sense, it's a sort of temporary excommunication.

86
00:11:35,990 --> 00:11:37,714
And the idea is for the

87
00:11:38,149 --> 00:11:43,755
for that monk to understand how important the sangha is, how important it is to live in community.

88
00:11:45,538 --> 00:11:54,589
And then to get back into the community, 20 monks have to be collected from all sorts of places.

89
00:11:55,230 --> 00:12:00,816
And the ceremony then is to receive the penitent back into the order.

90
00:12:00,855 --> 00:12:03,499
So it's a big to-do, you know.

91
00:12:04,524 --> 00:12:09,208
So these two monks are the cause of this particular rule.

92
00:12:09,909 --> 00:12:30,288
So it may be that a bhikkhu who is a corruptor of families by gifts and flowers, you know, by saying that I'll do special ceremonies for you, that it's a wonderful thing for you to offer me because I'm such a highly developed spiritual person and so on.

93
00:12:30,308 --> 00:12:34,312
And of bad behavior, lives in dependence on some village or town

94
00:12:34,832 --> 00:12:43,163
and that both his bad behavior is seen and heard about, and families corrupted by him are seen and heard about.

95
00:12:44,065 --> 00:12:47,068
That Bhikkhu should be admonished by Bhikkhus thus.

96
00:12:47,629 --> 00:12:51,575
The Venerable One is a corrupter of families and of bad behavior.

97
00:12:52,475 --> 00:13:01,768
Both the Venerable One's bad behavior is seen and heard about, and families corrupted by him are seen and heard about.

98
00:13:02,456 --> 00:13:04,818
Let the venerable ones leave this abode.

99
00:13:05,460 --> 00:13:07,600
You have lived here long enough.

100
00:13:08,802 --> 00:13:22,573
Should that Bhikkhu, being admonished by Bhikkhus, thus say to those Bhikkhus, the Bhikkhus are guided by desire, guided by hatred and guided by delusion and guided by fear.

101
00:13:23,375 --> 00:13:25,655
So they're saying you don't understand the situation.

102
00:13:25,697 --> 00:13:26,977
You're all deluded.

103
00:13:27,857 --> 00:13:32,261
On account of such a fault, they banish one and do not banish another.

104
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:36,265
Then that bhikkhu should be admonished by the bhikkhus thus.

105
00:13:37,047 --> 00:13:38,929
Let the venerable one not say so.

106
00:13:39,269 --> 00:13:48,684
The bhikkhus are not guided by desire, nor guided by hate, nor guided by delusion, nor guided by fear.

107
00:13:51,288 --> 00:13:55,514
The venerable one is a corrupter of families and of bad behaviour.

108
00:13:56,203 --> 00:14:03,315
Both the Venerable One's bad behavior is seen and heard about and families corrupted by him are seen and heard about.

109
00:14:04,096 --> 00:14:06,539
Let the Venerable One leave this abode.

110
00:14:06,559 --> 00:14:08,341
You have lived here long enough.

111
00:14:09,443 --> 00:14:22,683
Should that Bhikkhu on being admonished by Bhikkhus thus endeavor as before, then that Bhikkhu should be remonstrated with by Bhikkhus up to a third time so that he may relinquish his endeavor.

112
00:14:23,355 --> 00:14:30,307
If on being remonstrated with up to third time he does relinquish, that is good.

113
00:14:30,988 --> 00:14:36,517
If he should not relinquish, this entails initial and subsequent meeting of the community.

114
00:14:36,856 --> 00:14:39,581
So that's when he has to go into five months.

115
00:14:39,861 --> 00:14:47,854
Now, if he doesn't do that, of course, then he has to go and find, well, he can't go to that monastery.

116
00:14:47,874 --> 00:14:48,315
He's left.

117
00:14:48,455 --> 00:14:50,899
He's excommunicated.

118
00:14:52,195 --> 00:15:17,577
it's not a pleasant thing to happen so this is what happens to these two uh to these two monks you see and uh so um i do not say i do not say that all because that they still have work to do with diligence but i do say of all because that they have no work to do with diligence

119
00:15:18,115 --> 00:15:20,999
So here now there's a change of pace.

120
00:15:21,919 --> 00:15:27,126
A change of pace.

121
00:15:27,147 --> 00:15:47,193
I do not say that those bhikkhus who are arahats, those bhikkhus who've made the path with their tents destroyed, who've lived the holy life, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, reached the true goal, destroyed the fetters of becoming and are completely liberated through final knowledge

122
00:15:47,443 --> 00:15:49,768
that they have work to do with diligence.

123
00:15:50,830 --> 00:15:51,471
Why is that?

124
00:15:52,913 --> 00:15:54,655
They have done their work with diligence.

125
00:15:54,755 --> 00:15:57,620
They are no more capable of being negligent.

126
00:15:59,182 --> 00:16:02,067
Now I say of such bhikkhus are in the higher training.

127
00:16:03,610 --> 00:16:13,265
So that's the ones who have attained the first three attainments, the stream entrant, the want returner and the non-returner.

128
00:16:14,544 --> 00:16:26,275
Those whose minds have not yet reached the goal and who are still aspiring to the supreme security from bondage, that they still have work to do with diligence.

129
00:16:27,277 --> 00:16:27,976
Why is that?

130
00:16:28,158 --> 00:16:42,831
Because when those venerable ones make use of suitable resting places, associate with good friends and balance their spiritual faculties, they may be realizing for themselves with direct knowledge here and now

131
00:16:43,115 --> 00:17:12,902
enter upon and abide in the supreme goal of the holy life for the sake of which clansmen rightly go forth the home life into homelessness so um here we've got some idea of what's needed in order to uh for final for our final liberation so suitable resting places well that's that's pretty straightforward but associate with good friends dharma friends friends who are going to support us and to balance the spiritual faculties

132
00:17:12,882 --> 00:17:22,919
That is faith, effort, mindfulness, concentration and wisdom.

133
00:17:24,141 --> 00:17:26,124
So these have to be sort of balanced.

134
00:17:26,664 --> 00:17:29,769
And of course, that's what we're doing when we sit in meditation.

135
00:17:30,811 --> 00:17:32,535
We keep our attention steady.

136
00:17:33,696 --> 00:17:38,163
The confidence, the faith is already there in the practice.

137
00:17:38,566 --> 00:17:43,089
We're putting the effort in to remain mindful, to be aware.

138
00:17:43,871 --> 00:17:51,676
We're trying to keep that steadiness of attention, and we're doing the investigation into those three characteristics of existence.

139
00:17:52,458 --> 00:17:57,622
Impermanence, the cause of suffering, and not-self.

140
00:17:57,642 --> 00:18:07,611
So this is, although this is directed to the monastics, it has great meaning also for lay people.

141
00:18:08,688 --> 00:18:09,848
That's you.

142
00:18:12,632 --> 00:18:18,057
So don't make too much of a difference between lay people and the monastics.

143
00:18:18,739 --> 00:18:24,904
Now, just this evening, it's just good to just think about renunciation.

144
00:18:24,964 --> 00:18:32,232
Nobody wants to renounce things because there's a sense of loss.

145
00:18:32,894 --> 00:18:37,699
So, you know, really we have to impress upon ourselves that this renunciation,

146
00:18:37,949 --> 00:18:43,354
is not the destruction of the joys and the pleasures of life.

147
00:18:43,773 --> 00:18:48,698
It's to do with that relationship of attachment, of need, of compulsive behavior.

148
00:18:49,659 --> 00:19:03,971
And if we can tackle the worst one, something that we have a problem with, it might be eating, it might be whatever.

149
00:19:04,912 --> 00:19:07,534
I always say eating because everybody has this problem.

150
00:19:08,087 --> 00:19:08,949
one way or the other.

151
00:19:09,730 --> 00:19:16,318
And it's the case of just choosing something which you can see as a sort of compulsive thing.

152
00:19:17,119 --> 00:19:26,231
It could be just that one time in the day when you have to have the cup of tea or in the evening when you have to have that piece of toast when you know you shouldn't have that piece of toast.

153
00:19:27,212 --> 00:19:27,874
Something like that.

154
00:19:28,375 --> 00:19:36,224
And just to sort of sit there with it and just to feel the energy of that compulsive nature.

155
00:19:36,625 --> 00:19:37,487
Now remember,

156
00:19:37,905 --> 00:20:06,208
there are two reasons why we indulge the first one is because it's delightful in itself but more often than not it's because we're trying not to feel the underlying unhappiness and that's when we start eating for comfort we start eating for to make us feel happy when actually we're feeling depressed so it's a case of really beginning to investigate this whole business of indulgence and

157
00:20:07,218 --> 00:20:17,351
as we let go of it, you see, to really feel that sense of release, that sense of relief from something which is enchaining us.

158
00:20:17,691 --> 00:20:18,593
That's a fetter.

159
00:20:19,032 --> 00:20:26,883
That's a little, shall we say, indication of what it's like to finally let go of the whole damn thing.

160
00:20:28,125 --> 00:20:34,692
And that's the dropping away of this whole intention of trying to find any

161
00:20:35,061 --> 00:20:38,666
permanent, solid happiness in this form.

162
00:20:39,208 --> 00:20:41,872
And that's what we call Nibbāna.

163
00:20:41,892 --> 00:20:49,363
Nibbāna is simply the end of that shacklement, the unshacklement of our indulgence.

164
00:20:50,204 --> 00:20:53,710
So the Buddha here is constantly playing with rules.

165
00:20:53,789 --> 00:20:55,112
Every time a monk

166
00:20:55,446 --> 00:21:10,685
somebody or a nun breaks a rule, does something which is not, shall we say, within the monastic idea of renunciation, then you get a little rule coming up.

167
00:21:10,705 --> 00:21:12,647
So it's very pragmatic.

168
00:21:12,748 --> 00:21:14,369
It's very organic.

169
00:21:14,390 --> 00:21:18,315
There's no, if you ever get round to reading the rule,

170
00:21:18,582 --> 00:21:41,400
the rules are all over the place even though they're put in certain sections and that's because they came as people didn't behave well it is said that for the first 20 years or so such was the caliber of people joining that there were no need for rules they just followed the normal ways of renunciates

171
00:21:41,498 --> 00:21:49,391
which other orders did, like the James and other people who'd left the household life, as you say.

172
00:21:49,912 --> 00:22:00,710
But then after 20 years, the standard of people joining seems to have dropped a little bit, and in this case quite a lot, and therefore rules had to be established.

173
00:22:01,411 --> 00:22:04,215
And the rules make the institution, right?

174
00:22:04,256 --> 00:22:05,498
That's your institution.

175
00:22:05,900 --> 00:22:07,662
The Vinaya itself is the institution.

176
00:22:07,982 --> 00:22:10,405
And the Buddha himself said he left two things.

177
00:22:10,746 --> 00:22:15,852
This is the importance he gave to the Vinaya.

178
00:22:15,872 --> 00:22:21,138
He said he left the Dharma, which is both the understanding of the practice and the Vinaya.

179
00:22:21,660 --> 00:22:29,288
And he saw that the order of monks and nuns were to be, shall we say, the ground of his dispensation.

180
00:22:30,590 --> 00:22:34,776
So with that in mind and the whole

181
00:22:35,009 --> 00:23:01,354
idea of renunciation, just to spend some time reflecting on what it means to renounce these unwholesome desires and to go through that barrier of letting go, to feel the relief, the release and the joy of being liberated from a certain psychological need, an unnecessary psychological need.

182
00:23:02,244 --> 00:23:16,173
So I can only hope that this discourse has been of some assistance and that you will, of course, through the process of liberating yourself from these shackles, will attain the great goal sooner rather than later.

183
00:23:20,041 --> 00:23:22,026
So we can do a little practice.

184
00:23:37,462 --> 00:24:01,294
Poryan pujheni Raman pujheni Sanghan pujheni

185
00:24:06,606 --> 00:24:08,736
Namo Tassa.

